\s12 \li1463 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \f0 \b \fs52 NSFontManager\
\fs80\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s31 \li4231 \fi-2719 \ri1007 \ql \tx4233 \f0 \b \fs24 Inherits From:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 NSObject\
\fs20\
\pard \s21 \li4233 \fi-2721 \ri1007 \ql \tx4233 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 Conforms To:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 NSObject\'80(NSObject)\
\fs20\
\s22 \li4233 \fi-2721 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 Declared In:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 AppKit/NSFontManager.h\
\fs20\
\fs20\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s10 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description\
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 NSFontManager is the center of activity for the font conversion system. It records the currently selected font, updates the Font Panel and Font menu to reflect the selected font, initiates font changes, and converts fonts in response to requests from text-bearing objects. In a more prosaic role, NSFontManager can be queried for the fonts available to the application, and for the particular attributes of a font, such as whether it\'27s condensed or extended.\
\fs14\
\fs28 You normally set up a font manager and the Font Menu using Interface Builder. However, you can also do so programmatically by getting the shared font manager instance and having it create the standard Font menu at run time:\
\fs14\
\pard \s20 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 NSFontManager *fontManager = [NSFontManager sharedFontManager];\
\fi0 NSMenu *fontMenu = [fontManager fontMenu:YES];\
\fs14\
\f1 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 You can then add the Font menu to your application\'27s main menu. Once the Font menu is installed, your application automatically gains the functionality of both the Font menu and the Font Panel.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s61 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Recording the Selected Font\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Any object that records fonts that the user can change should tell the font manager what the font of its selection is whenever it becomes the first responder and whenever its selection changes while it\'27s the first responder. The object does so by sending the shared font manager a \b setSelectedFont:isMultiple:\b0  message. It should pass in the first font of the selection, along with a flag indicating whether there\'27s more than one font.\
\fs14\
\fs28 The font manager uses this information to update the Font Panel and Font menu to reflect the selected font. For example, suppose the selected font is set as Helvetica Oblique 12.0 point. In this case the Font Panel selects that font and displays its name; the Font menu changes its Italic command to Unitalic; if there\'27s no Bold variant of Helvetica available, the Bold menu item is disabled; and so on.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s61 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Initiating Font Changes\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The user normally changes the font of the selection by manipulating the Font Panel and Font menu. These objects initiate the intended change by sending an action message to the font manager. There are four font-changing action methods:\
\fs14\
\pard \s4 \li2338 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2338 \tx3753 \tx4146 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80addFontTrait:\
\fi0 \f3 -\f1 \'80removeFontTrait:\
\fi0 \f3 -\f1 \'80modifyFont:\
\fi0 \f3 -\f1 \'80modifyFontViaPanel:\
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 The first three cause the font manager to query the sender of the message in order to determine which trait to add or remove, or how to modify the font. The last causes the font manager to use the settings in the Font Panel to modify the font. The font manager records this information and uses it in later requests to convert fonts, as described under \'aaResponding to Font Changes.\'ba\
\fs14\
\fs28 When the font manager receives an \b addFontTrait:\b0  or \b removeFontTrait:\b0  message, it queries the sender with a \b tag\b0  message, interpreting the return value as a trait mask for use with \b convertFont:toHaveTrait:\b0  or \b convertFont:toNotHaveTrait:\b0 , as described below under \'aaConverting Fonts Manually.\'ba The Italic and Bold Font menu commands, for example, have tags of NSItalicFontMask and NSBoldFontMask, respectively. See \b convertFont:toHaveTrait:\b0  for a list of trait mask values.\
\fs14\
\fs28 When the font manager receives a \b modifyFont:\b0  message, it queries the sender with a \b tag\b0  message and interprets the return value as a particular kind of conversion to perform, via the various conversion methods described under \'aaConverting Fonts Manually.\'ba For example, a button whose tag value is NSSizeUpFontAction causes the font manager\'27s \b convertFont:\b0  method to increase the size of the NSFont passed as the argument. See \b modifyFont:\b0  for a list of conversion tag values.\
\fs14\
\fs28 For \b modifyFontViaPanel:\b0 , the font manager sends the application\'27s Font Panel a \b panelConvertFont:\b0  message. The Font Panel in turn uses the font manager to convert the font provided according to the user\'27s choices. For example, if the user selects only the font family in the Font Panel (perhaps to Helvetica), then whatever fonts are provided to \b panelConvertFont:\b0 , only the family is changed: Courier Medium 10.0 point becomes Helvetica Medium 10.0 point, while Times Italic 12.0 point becomes Helvetica Oblique 12.0 point.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s61 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Responding to Font Changes\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The font manager responds to a font-changing action method by sending a \b changeFont:\b0  action message up the responder chain. A text-bearing object that receives this message should have the font manager convert the fonts in its selection by invoking \b convertFont:\b0  for each font and using the NSFont object returned. \b convertFont:\b0  uses the information recorded by the font-changing action method, such as \b addFontTrait:\b0 , modifying the font provided appropriately. (There\'27s no way to explicitly set the font-changing action or trait; instead, you use the methods described under \'aaConverting Fonts Manually.\'ba)\
\fs14\
\fs28 This simple example assumes there\'27s only one font in the selection:\
\fs14\
\pard \s20 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 \f3 -\f2 \'80(void)changeFont:(id)sender\
\fi0 \{\
\fi0     NSFont *oldFont = [self selectionFont];\
\fi0     NSFont *newFont = [sender convertFont:oldFont];\
\fi0     [self setSelectionFont:newFont];\
\fi0     return;\
\fi0 \}\
\fs14\
\f1 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Most text-bearing objects will have to scan the selection for ranges with different fonts, and invoke \b convertFont:\b0  for each one.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s61 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Font Trait Masks\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 NSFontManager categorizes fonts according to a small set of traits. You can convert fonts by adding and removing individual traits, and you can get a font with a specific combination of traits. The traits defined and available for your use are:\
\fs14\
\pard \s63 \li6753 \fi-4838 \ri1007 \ql \tx6753 \tx9525 \fs28 NSItalicFontMask\tab NSCondensedFontMask\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSBoldFontMask\tab NSExpandedFontMask\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSNarrowFontMask\tab NSCompressedFontMask\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSFixedPitchFontMask\tab NSSmallCapsFontMask\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSPosterFontMask\
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 NSCondensedFontMask and NSExpandedFontMask are mutually exclusive. In addition to these, NSFontManager defines the masks NSUnitalicFontMask, NSUnboldFontMask, and NSNonStandardCharacterSetFontMask for its own internal use. Though they\'27re defined in the header file, you shouldn\'27t use these masks.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s61 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Converting Fonts Manually\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 NSFontManager defines a number of methods for explicitly converting particular traits and characteristics of a font. These methods are:\
\fs14\
\pard \s4 \li2338 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2338 \tx3753 \tx4146 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80convertFont:toFace:\
\fi0 \f3 -\f1 \'80convertFont:toFamily:\
\fi0 \f3 -\f1 \'80convertFont:toHaveTrait:\
\fi0 \f3 -\f1 \'80convertFont:toNotHaveTrait:\
\fi0 \f3 -\f1 \'80convertFont:toSize:\
\fi0 \f3 -\f1 \'80convertWeight:ofFont:\
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 Each returns a transformed version of the font provided, or the original font if it can\'27t be converted. \b convertFont:toFace:\b0  and \b convertFont:toFamily:\b0  both alter the basic design of the font provided. The first method requires a fully-specified typeface name, such as \'aaTimes-Roman\'ba or \'aaHelvetica-BoldOblique\'ba, while the second expects only a family name, such as \'aaTimes\'ba or \'aaHelvetica\'ba.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \b convertFont:toHaveTrait:\b0  and \b convertFont:toNotHaveTrait:\b0  use trait masks to add or remove a single trait such as Italic, Bold, Condensed, or Extended.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \b convertFont:toSize:\b0  returns a font of the requested size, with all other characteristics the same as those of the original font.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \b convertWeight:ofFont:\b0  either increases or decreases the weight of the font provided, according to a boolean flag. Font weights are typically indicated by a series of names, which can vary from font to font. Some go from Light to Medium to Bold, while others have Book, SemiBold, Bold, and Black. This method offers a uniform way of incrementing and decrementing any font\'27s weight.\
\fs14\
\fs28 The default implementation of font conversion is very conservative, making a change only if no other trait or aspect is affected. For example, if you try to convert Helvetica Oblique 12.0 point by adding the Bold trait, and only Helvetica Bold is available, the font isn\'27t converted. You can create a subclass of NSFontManager and override the conversion methods to perform less conservative conversion, perhaps using Helvetica Bold in this case and losing the Oblique trait.\
\fs14\
\fs28 In addition to the font-conversion methods, NSFontManager defines \b fontWithFamily:traits:weight:size:\b0  to construct a font with a given set of characteristics. If you don\'27t care to make a subclass of NSFontManager, you can use this method to perform approximate font conversions yourself.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s61 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Examining Fonts\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 In addition to converting fonts, NSFontManager provides information on which fonts are available to the application, and on the characteristics of any given font. \b availableFonts\b0  returns an array of the names of all fonts available. \b availableFontNamesWithTraits:\b0  filters the available fonts based on a font trait mask.\
\fs14\
\fs28 There are three methods for examining individual fonts. \b fontNamed:hasTraits:\b0  returns YES if the font matches the trait mask provided. \b traitsOfFont:\b0  returns a trait mask for a given font. \b weightOfFont:\b0  returns an approximate ranking of a font\'27s weight on a scale of 0\f3 -\f1 15, where 0 is the lightest possible weight, 5 is Normal or Book weight, 9 is the equivalent of Bold, and 15 is the heaviest possible (often called Black or Ultra Black).\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s61 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Customizing the Font Conversion System\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 If you need to customize the font conversion system by creating subclasses of NSFontManager or NSFontPanel, you must inform the NSFontManager class of this change with a \b setFontManagerFactory:\b0  or \b setFontPanelFactory:\b0  message, before either the shared font manager or shared font panel is created. These methods record your class as the one to instantiate the first time the font manager or Font Panel is requested.\
\fs14\
\fs28 You may be able to avoid using subclasses if all you need is to add some custom controls to the Font Panel. In this case, you can invoke NSFontPanel\'27s \b setAccessoryView:\b0  method to add an NSView below its font browser. This allows you to add controls for such things as font color, kerning, and so on.\
\fs14\
\fs28 If you provide your own Font menu, you should register it with the font manager using the \b setFontMenu:\b0  method. The font manager is responsible for validating Font menu items and changing their titles and tags according to the selected font. For example, when the selected font is Italic the font manager changes the Italic Font menu item to Unitalic, and changes its tag to NSUnitalicFontMask. Your Font menu\'27s items should use the appropriate action methods and tags. Here are some examples:\
\fs14\
\pard \s65 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx6753 \tx9553 \f0 \b \fs28 Font Menu Item\tab Action\tab Tag\
\fs6\
\pard \s63 \li6753 \fi-4838 \ri1007 \ql \tx6753 \tx9525 \f1 \b0 \fs28 Italic\tab \b addFontTrait:\b0 \tab NSItalicFontMask\
\fs14\
\fs28 Bold\tab \b addFontTrait:\b0 \tab NSBoldFontMask\
\fs14\
\fs28 Heavier\tab \b modifyFont:\b0 \tab NSHeavierFontAction\
\fs14\
\fs28 Larger\tab \b modifyFont:\b0 \tab NSSizeUpFontAction\
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 Normally, the application\'27s Font Panel displays all the standard fonts available on the system. If this isn\'27t appropriate for your application\'d0for example, if only fixed-pitch fonts should be used\'d0you can assign a delegate to the font manager object to filter the available fonts. Before the Font Panel displays a particular font family or face, it asks the font manager to confirm it. The font manager in turn queries its delegate with a \b fontManager:willIncludeFont:\b0  message. If the delegate returns YES (or doesn\'27t implement this method), the font is displayed in the Font Panel. If the delegate returns NO, the font isn\'27t listed.\
\fs14\
\fs56\
